# THE BUILD CONTEXT IS NOT THE REPOSITORY.
#
# Every Dockerfile here is invoked with the repo root as context (`docker build -f
# ./backend/Dockerfile .`, which is what `.github/workflows/deploy-{dev,staging,production}.yml`
# run). Without this file Docker tars the ENTIRE tree and uploads it before the first layer is
# created. In this repository that is a 1.2 GB upload whose composition is, in order of size,
# `node_modules` (1.2 GB), `frontend/.next` (126 MB), `.git` (19 MB), `backend/coverage` (15 MB),
# `backend/dist` (6 MB) — none of which is an input to any image. Three concrete failures follow
# from sending them anyway, and the first two already happened in this repository:
#
#   1. `COPY backend/ ./` lays the HOST `node_modules` on top of the image's fresh `npm ci`
#      output. The image then runs whatever the developer happened to have installed locally
#      instead of what the committed lockfile resolves to, and no build error reports it. The
#      image is not reproducible by construction.
#   2. The same `COPY backend/ ./` lays `backend/.env` and `backend/.env.production.template`
#      into image layers. A layer is not removed by `rm` in a later layer — the secret is in
#      the image, readable by anyone who can pull it, forever. Principle #15 (proactive defense
#      first) says the exclusion IS the defense; deleting after baking is not a control.
#   3. Minutes of tar and upload per build, on every build, for content that is never used.
#
# RULE FOR EDITING THIS FILE: every `COPY` in every Dockerfile must still resolve after a new
# exclusion is added, and nothing that carries a secret may be re-included. The current COPY
# sources are, exhaustively: root `package.json`, root `package-lock.json`,
# `packages/shared-types/package.json`, `packages/shared-types/` (source + tsconfig),
# `backend/package.json`, `backend/` (source + tsconfig + nest-cli.json + .swcrc),
# `frontend/package.json`, `frontend/` (source + next.config.ts + postcss config + public),
# and `frontend/scripts/` — not merely copied but EXECUTED inside the builder stage, by the
# `RUN npm run verify:csp --workspace=frontend` assertion in `frontend/Dockerfile`. A path that
# a build runs is a stricter dependency than one it only reads: a future context-trimming
# exclusion here does not surface as a missing file in a COPY, it surfaces as
# `Cannot find module '…/frontend/scripts/verify-csp.mjs'` failing the image build with nothing
# in the diff to explain it. Keep it.

# --- Dependencies ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The image installs its own from the lockfile inside its own stage. The host tree is never an
# input to an image; including it is what breaks reproducibility (failure 1 above).
**/node_modules

# --- Build output ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Every image rebuilds its own output inside its own stage, deliberately:
#   packages/shared-types/dist  built by `npm run build --workspace=packages/shared-types`
#   backend/dist                built by `nest build`
#   frontend/.next              built by `next build`
# Shipping the host's stale output would either be overwritten anyway, or worse, mask a build
# failure — an image that appears to build while carrying the previous machine's result.
**/dist
**/.next
**/build

# --- Test, coverage and tool output ------------------------------------------------------------
**/coverage
**/playwright-report
**/test-results
**/.playwright
**/e2e-results.json
**/*.tsbuildinfo
**/.turbo
**/.eslintcache

# --- Version control and CI metadata ------------------------------------------------------------
# Never an input to an image. `.git` alone is ~19 MB of context, and `.github` is CI behaviour,
# not build input. (`.gitignore` is NOT matched by the `.git` pattern — the names differ.)
**/.git
**/.github

# --- Secrets ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHY `.env` AND `.env.*` BOTH: `.env` alone would still admit `backend/.env.production` and
# `frontend/.env.production.template`, which are exactly the files that carry production-shaped
# values. No Dockerfile in this repository references an env file — each is written to be
# configured from the container environment (Compose `environment:`, or the Helm chart's
# values/secret), so there is nothing here to keep.
#
# `**/.env.example` is deliberately NOT excepted back in. A build that needs it has no business
# existing (an example file is documentation, not build input), and the cost of being wrong in
# that direction is zero, while the cost of a mistaken `!` exception on the secret side is a
# rotation. If a future Dockerfile genuinely needs a template, that is a `COPY` of a named,
# reviewed file — not a blanket re-inclusion of `*.env.*`.
**/.env
**/.env.*

# --- Logs ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
**/*.log
**/npm-debug.log*
**/logs

# --- Editor and OS junk --------------------------------------------------------------------------
**/.DS_Store
**/Thumbs.db
**/.idea
**/.vscode
**/*.swp
**/*~

# --- Re-included: build-EXECUTED paths -----------------------------------------------------------
# WHY A GUARD AND NOT JUST A NOTE. The `!` rules below have to come AFTER every exclusion they
# could contradict, because Docker matches patterns in order and a later negation only wins over
# an earlier match — the reverse order silently keeps the file out. Nothing above currently
# excludes these paths, so this is not a fix for a live bug; it is the line that stops a future
# context-trimming rule (`**/scripts`, say, added to shave the upload) from breaking
# `frontend/Dockerfile`'s `RUN npm run verify:csp --workspace=frontend` with an ENOENT that names
# no file in the diff that caused it.
#
# This is a re-inclusion of BUILD CODE, which is a different category from the `**/.env*` note
# above: no exclusion here can be widened to reach a secret without removing its own block, and
# `verify-csp.mjs` is committed, dependency-free (node builtins only) and reviewed like the
# Dockerfile that runs it. Never put a `!` rule in the secrets section.
!frontend/scripts
!frontend/scripts/**
